  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The bullpen held when it mattered most, and the lineup finally found a late answer.",
    "what_this_means": "Cortes flipped a one-run deficit in the eighth with a home run that turned a chase into a lead. That matters because this team has spent the season hunting moments like this. Late chances that feel thin until someone converts one. The rout started early; Angels scored four in the third and the game looked decided. But the lineup didn't accept it. Over the sixth and seventh innings, they clawed back with runners in scoring position finally producing. Butler and Langeliers did the early damage with two hits and two RBI apiece. Blewett and Johnson held the middle innings clean while the bats caught up. This is what narrow wins look like when they arrive late: incomplete until the final swing, then suddenly credible. The formula broke down once this season. This time it held.",
    "what_to_watch": "Walbert Ure\u00f1a will take the mound tonight for the Angels with a 2.74 ERA, but their lineup is hitting .234. The kind of offense Jeffrey Springs can work against if the Oakland bats stay awake past the fourth inning.",
